A||N||13||sully5||86SE rear brakes||||5||
Z||000000||sully5||02-26-2000||11:58 AM||||I just got my first Fiero - a 1986SE and it is in dire need of a brake job. I have never worked on one of these and was just wondering if there are any problem areas I should watch out for when I change the pads? Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||02-26-2000||12:30 PM||||Okay, having ruined a couple of calipers already... heres the scoop. First off, do NOT try to push the pistons in with a C-clamp.  After a couple of cranks you will hear a loud "clnk-snap" noise and then the sound of money leaving your wallet.  The pistons on the rear are the "screw-in" type that requires a special tool to turn in. There are a couple of slots in the face for an adapter to go in that you turn witha ratchet. Make sure that you don't lose any bits and pieces, and if you ever need to change a brake line, be very careful starting the fittings as the calipers are made of aluminum that is only slightly harder than cold butter.<BR>Hope this helps.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sully5||02-26-2000||12:34 PM||||Thanks for the info stimpy. You just saved me a bundle. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-26-2000||01:00 PM||||The Correct procedure for compressing Fiero Rear callipers.<P>Remove the e-brake nut and arm from the back of the caliper. Hold the arm still while turning the nut.<P>Place a large socket over the E-brake actuator bolt.<P>compress the piston into the calliper using a large C clamp.<P><BR>Inspect the actuator seal. the caliper MUST be replaced/repaired if any leakage is present.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||02-26-2000||02:06 PM||||Not to dispute the acknowleged technical expertise of theogre... but the little slotted adapter cost me $7 at an auto parts store. A new caliper cost far more.  Sure, you can eventually press a piston in, but that is not how it was designed to be done. Also, no matter what technique you use, the piston will spin as it sets into the caliper. You can use the handy little adapter to get the slots for the clip that holds the pad on to their correct orientation. Or you can knock them into place with a screwdriver and a big f-ing hammer. <BR>Really, the correct tool for the job ain't that expensive.<BR>theogre, am I correct in deducing that you had to replace your calipers after doing the job using the ol' C-clamp method? Maybe that snap I heard when I f*cked up my calipers was that actuator seal you were talking about?  I just don't see how the brakes would work with a broken seal...certainly not well enough to wear down a set of pads.  Not flaming, just thinking out loud.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shiner||02-26-2000||03:11 PM||||I concur with Ogre on this one.  The special tool might be nice and all, but this method is easy as heck.  This is how I have done both of my fieros, and it works fine and dandy.  Besides, you don't want to make Ogre mad!  If it wasn't for him, most of our cars probably wouldn't be running!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||02-26-2000||06:40 PM||||Ho boy! Shiner, don't get me in trouble again! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I certainly am (underlned, capitilized, italcized) NOT trying do make anyone mad. I would like to know what I did to destroy a set of calipers.  I have always used the C-clamp method, and on the front it was easy money. When I went to do the rear brakes, I did it exactly as theogre illustrauted.  After 2 or 3 turns of the clamp, I heard the hefty snap noise and the cylinder collapsed.  Problem was, it would not stay collapsed, and I could not for the life of me get the pads and caliper back on the rotor. I duuno, maybe I was born under a bad sign, but using that method was VERY expensive for me.<BR>Let me review, right tool for the job -$7.  Using the "inexpensive method" (assuming that you already have that $17 mega c-clamp) cost right around $170 dollars. Showing up stimpy- priceless. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You can do the math however you like. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||02-26-2000||06:47 PM||||I have to agree with Stimpy on this one. When I replaced one of my rear calipers. I had to transfer over the pads. I had a c-clamp, didn't use it. I did not have the little $7 magical tool either. I used a pair of hose clamp pliers to twist the piston back into the cylinder bore. Might not be the best/safest way to do the job, but I plan on doing a complete brake job in the months ahead.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||02-26-2000||06:58 PM||||I sawed the ends off of an old brake pad welded on a handle and ta da a winder inner tool||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rodrv6||02-27-2000||12:18 AM||||Okay, I guess I'll jump in on this one.  The method the Ogre describes is the same as the one in the factory shop manual.  I have tried doing it both ways, and while I've never had any problems with either method, I much prefer using the tool to turn the piston back into the caliper.  That allows you to avoid having to remove the parking brake lever from the caliper and messing with the 2 washers that go between the lever and the caliper.  It's also easy to allow the parking brake screw to get pushed into the caliper when the lever is off, and then you have to bleed it since air will get in.<BR>On the other hand, if you turn the piston to retract it, you have to be careful not to twist the dust seal boot and mess it up.  Either way works, and either way has it's potential problems.||||reg||10
Z||000010||sully5||02-27-2000||07:57 PM||||Just wanted to say thanks a bunch for all the good advice. I got one of those tools from a friend of mine and it seemed to work(I will keep the c-clamp method in mind, too). Of course, just when everything seemed to be going fine, the bleeder valve snaped off when I tried to loosen it. No telling how long it had been since the system had been serviced. Well, better get back to the garage. Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000011||sdm1964||02-27-2000||08:46 PM||||This is too weired, i just got done putting brakes on the rear of the 856GT, I bought a  special tool. I had no problems turned right in, The one problem i had is that the samll dust boots for the bolts were missing and making the calpier loose on the disc. It seems that we need to relace the o-rings and boots to keep eveything tight. It just seems some methods work for some and not for others, like the ATF in the clutch thingy..||||reg||14
Z||000012||sdm1964||02-27-2000||08:46 PM||||This is too weired, i just got done putting brakes on the rear of the 856GT, I bought a  special tool. I had no problems turned right in, The one problem i had is that the samll dust boots for the bolts were missing and making the calpier loose on the disc. It seems that we need to relace the o-rings and boots to keep eveything tight. It just seems some methods work for some and not for others, like the ATF in the clutch thingy..||||reg||14
Z||000013||stsmithgt||02-28-2000||03:42 PM||||One way to save money is using the tool called, common sense.  Be aware of the force the C-clamp is putting on the piston so if there is a problem, you can catch it before "ping! snap!" Become one with your tools grasshopper. With aluminum there is such thing as too tight.||||reg||1
